<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFrameworks>net481;net9.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>NekoLib.Devices.RuntimeTests.Com0Com</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>NekoLib.Devices.RuntimeTests.Com0Com</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One executable in two paths.

    Without a mode flag it is the original oracle parity run: it opens the
    client ends of the com0com pairs and talks to the independent
    NekoPcbEmulator. That path is unchanged, so its 2026-08-01 evidence still
    describes what the binary does.

    With a mode flag it is the E3-DEV controller: it opens BOTH ends of the
    pairs, acting as its own peer, and drives smoke, recovery rehearsal and
    soak against faults the independent oracle cannot be asked to produce.
    The two are mutually exclusive by construction, because both want the
    emulator's ports.

    System.IO.Ports arrives transitively from NekoLib.Devices on net9.0 and is
    in the framework on net481, so the scenario-owned peer needs no package of
    its own.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\src\Devices\NekoLib.Devices\NekoLib.Devices.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Shared\NekoLib.RuntimeTests.Harness\NekoLib.RuntimeTests.Harness.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
